and the plane \n$z=4$  onto the $xy$-plane is an ellipse. What are the lengths of the semi-axes of the ellipse?" :question/worked-solution "To find the intersection of the paraboloid and the plane $z=4$, we substitute $z=4$ into the equation of the paraboloid, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(x-5)^{2}+6(y+4)^{2}=z+8 \\\\ 4(x-5)^{2}+6(y+4)^{2}=4+8 \\\\ 4(x-5)^{2}+6(y+4)^{2}=12 \\\\ \\frac{(x-5)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-5)^{2}}{(\\sqrt{3})^{2}}+\\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{(\\sqrt{2})^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of an ellipse with semi-axes of lengths $\\sqrt{3}$ and $\\sqrt{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418046 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124556 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dff86a8b-7966-53f7-a7bc-287990fb611d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Traces of Paraboloids"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "019726e7-4913-502a-a919-de71561128cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{3}$ and $\\sqrt{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418040} {:answer/id #uuid "31103cb3-8db8-5966-8f8f-150d81076c20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ and $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418041} {:answer/id #uuid "3a2b823c-f5c0-585b-b3ff-0eec4bcfc2e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{3}$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418042} {:answer/id #uuid "9e786335-093c-5020-9d1b-5216151e0ab3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418043} {:answer/id #uuid "ce083963-6a81-5daf-90c1-ac994c70d5be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{3}$ and $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418044}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3a2b823c-f5c0-585b-b3ff-0eec4bcfc2e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{3}$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418042} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a4ae1c8-63c4-5962-bc3f-7d5f1634e01e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418045}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5ea54c52-c613-575d-9b4a-ae98c73f35ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-35926" :question/problem "The projection of the intersection between the hyperbolic paraboloid $12z=4(x+2)^{2}-3{y}^{2}$ and the plane \n$z=1$  onto the $xy$-plane is a hyperbola. What are the lengths of the semi-axes of the hyperbola?" :question/worked-solution "To find the intersection of the paraboloid and the plane $z=1$, we substitute $z=1$ into the equation of the paraboloid, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12z & =4(x+2)^{2}-3{y}^{2} \\\\ 12(1) & =4(x+2)^{2}-3{y}^{2} \\\\ 12 & =4(x+2)^{2}-3{y}^{2} \\\\ 1 & =\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{3}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} \\\\ 1 & =\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{(\\sqrt{3})^{2}}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of a hyperbola with semi-axes of lengths $\\sqrt{3}$ and $2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323729 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124556 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dff86a8b-7966-53f7-a7bc-287990fb611d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Traces of Paraboloids"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "205d1a65-04f6-59a3-a974-5b778afaa91e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321391} {:answer/id #uuid "4dcfa493-b877-5976-8c32-70d3d57ac828" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-2,3)$ in the $xy$-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321760} {:answer/id #uuid "5f6e9f3f-adaa-5df3-bb70-cd9f70742907" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-2,-3)$ in the $xy$-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321898} {:answer/id #uuid "e24be124-29de-5243-8c6e-8306d6f59e5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(2,-3)$ in the $xy$-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322915} {:answer/id #uuid "eccdcbac-973f-5f39-80bc-76abea742d02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(2,3)$ in the $xy$-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323016}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5f6e9f3f-adaa-5df3-bb70-cd9f70742907" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-2,-3)$ in the $xy$-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321898} :answer-field/id #uuid "e2f3b7e6-356f-5e12-ac50-e4c8413a78be" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323618}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "04c0f5ae-99dd-5b51-8d08-843351e49a3d" :question/math-academy-id "q-35930" :question/problem "The projection of the intersection between the elliptic paraboloid $5(x+2)^{2}+3(y+3)^{2}=z$ and the plane $z=2$  onto the $xy$-plane is an ellipse. Find the center of this ellipse." :question/worked-solution "To find the intersection of the paraboloid and the plane $z=2$, we substitute $z=2$ into the equation of the paraboloid, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(x+2)^{2}+3(y+3)^{2} & =z \\\\ 5(x+2)^{2}+3(y+3)^{2} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{2/5}+\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{2/3} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of an ellipse in the $xy$-plane that has its center at $(-2,-3).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366233 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124505 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9078ab1-37a4-5e96-ae12-a83d5cd8804e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Properties of a Hyperbolic Paraboloid"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7b4441e7-9ebc-5545-9efa-2ee1440fa1b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0,0)$ and $(0,4,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366235} {:answer/id #uuid "6bc6b114-2ad4-5286-98ef-bab6af5d1a58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,0,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366236} {:answer/id #uuid "2b746e61-be9d-59cb-8bf8-3b68b34626f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,-4,0)$ and $(0,0,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366237} {:answer/id #uuid "e590b348-2055-5203-8c91-c618c41626da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,-4,0)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366238} {:answer/id #uuid "84bf4e2c-448d-5b40-8003-6a09149d5ddd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,-2,0)$ and $(0,0,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366239}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2b746e61-be9d-59cb-8bf8-3b68b34626f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,-4,0)$ and $(0,0,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366237} :answer-field/id #uuid "98778e41-d388-55c7-822d-6a1e70584ae6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366234}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "16658c9e-8fa3-5c30-87d5-30c0a6e7db83" :question/math-academy-id "q-72302" :question/problem "Given the hyperbolic paraboloid $(x-6)^{2}-8(y+2)^{2}=z+4$, find its intercepts with the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "To find the $y$-intercepts of the paraboloid, we substitute $x=0$ and $z=0$ in the equation of the paraboloid:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0-6)^{2}-8(y+2)^{2} & =0+4 \\\\ 36-8(y+2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ 32 & =8(y+2)^{2} \\\\ (y+2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ y+2 & =\\pm 2 \\\\ y & =-2\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the solutions $y=-4$ and $y=0.$\n\nTherefore, the intercepts with the $y$-axis are $(0,-4,0)$ and $(0,0,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339088 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124505 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9078ab1-37a4-5e96-ae12-a83d5cd8804e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Properties of a Hyperbolic Paraboloid"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c67b3b1-ca17-50d9-826b-aa84c548da4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-7,0,0)$ and $(1,0,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336743} {:answer/id #uuid "767a3d83-a217-545e-829c-803f0a2e5f93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,0,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337098} {:answer/id #uuid "7d9dcfbc-5db8-52dc-aa70-3ce3548a031a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-7,0,0)$ and $(-1,0,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337168} {:answer/id #uuid "cd3c3792-dfb7-53e4-8d4f-f13a6930b54d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-7,0,0)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337832} {:answer/id #uuid "fb10441f-5dc2-53c4-8a56-253eb94e9ba8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,0,0)$ and $(7,0,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338235}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4c67b3b1-ca17-50d9-826b-aa84c548da4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-7,0,0)$ and $(1,0,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336743} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a0d88c8-d602-5247-ade9-89205172c789" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338432}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0b363aae-9a1a-5bd8-a7ce-6affaf56df43" :question/math-academy-id "q-72305" :question/problem "Given the hyperbolic paraboloid $(x+3)^{2}-2(y-3)^{2}=z-2$, find its intercepts with the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "To find the $x$-intercepts of the paraboloid, we substitute $y=0$ and $z=0$ in the equation of the paraboloid:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3)^{2}-2(0-3)^{2} & =0-2 \\\\ (x+3)^{2}-18 & =-2 \\\\ (x+3)^{2} & =16 \\\\ x+3 & =\\pm 4 \\\\ x & =-3\\pm 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the solutions $x=-7$ and $x=1.$\n\nTherefore, the intercepts with the $x$-axis are $(-7,0,0)$ and $(1,0,0).$"}]}
